Keep the pre-init literals in the RAM text section.

The reset hook runs from RAM and loads three literals with l32r,
which reaches only a literal placed before the instruction. Upstream
names the RAM text section in one global_asm block and emits the
literals in the next. The compiler does not keep the section from
one block to the next, so the literals land in the flash literal
pool, behind the code that loads them, and the link stops with
"l32r: literal placed after use". One block now names the section,
emits the literals and restores the section. The section is right
for the ESP32-S3 alone, which is the one chip this tree builds, so a
build for another chip stops with an error.

Applies to esp-hal 1.2.2 from crates.io; the pin and this patch are
consumed by nix/patched-crates.nix.

--- a/src/soc/mod.rs
+++ b/src/soc/mod.rs
@@ -191,13 +191,19 @@
         )
     }
 
-    #[cfg(esp32s3)]
-    global_asm!(".section .rwtext,\"ax\",@progbits");
+    // The section directive and the literals share one block, so the
+    // literals land in the literal pool of the section named here.
+    #[cfg(not(esp32s3))]
+    compile_error!(
+        "This copy of esp-hal is patched for the ESP32-S3 only. Read patches/esp-hal-pre-init-literals.patch before you build for another chip."
+    );
     global_asm!(
         "
+        .pushsection .rwtext,\"ax\",@progbits
         .literal sym_stack_chk_guard, {__stack_chk_guard}
         .literal stack_guard_value, {stack_guard_value}
         .literal sym_esp32_init, {__esp32_init}
+        .popsection
         ",
         __stack_chk_guard = sym __stack_chk_guard,
         stack_guard_value = const esp_config::esp_config_int!(
